.TH GLAZE-COMPOSITOR 1 "2026-11-10" "Glaze 0.1.0" "Glaze User Commands"
.SH NAME
glaze-compositor \- Glaze's native Wayland compositor
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B glaze-compositor
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B glaze-compositor
is Glaze's own Smithay-based Wayland compositor. It is the Stage 2 element of
the staged compositor strategy described in
.BR docs/adr/ADR-0002-compositor-strategy.md :
an existing wlroots compositor (Sway) provides real window state for the first
runnable shell, while the native compositor is implemented protocol by
protocol behind the same
.BR glaze_common::model
types.
.PP
.B IMPLEMENTATION STATUS:
the compositor backend is not functional yet, and the command is honest about
that rather than pretending to start a session.
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
On a non-Linux host it prints that the native compositor requires
Linux/Wayland and exits with status 1.
.IP \(bu 2
On Linux it calls the backend, which currently returns an
.B unsupported
error explaining that the Smithay backend is not yet implemented and that the
Sway/layer-shell backend should be used instead; the process then exits with
status 1.
.RE
.PP
The crate compiles and unit-tests its portable surface (capability reporting
and the protocol milestone table) on every host. Every protocol milestone -
.BR xdg-shell ,
.BR layer-shell ,
.BR outputs ,
.BR input ,
.BR seat ,
.BR foreign-toplevel ,
.BR xdg-decoration
and
.B XWayland
\- is currently in the
.B planned
state.
.SH OPTIONS
None. The command takes no options and reads no configuration.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
A clean shutdown. Not reachable until the backend is implemented.
.TP
.B 1
Returned on every host today: non-Linux prints an unsupported-host message, and
Linux reports that the backend is not yet implemented.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR glaze-session (1),
.BR glaze-shell (1),
.BR glaze-service (1),
.BR glaze (1),
.BR glaze-settings (1)
